.TH DANBY-LIMITSTOGROWTH 1
.SH NAME
danby-limitstogrowth \- Club of Rome System Dynamics model of population, capital, pollution, and resources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/limitstogrowth
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Danby/limitstogrowth
is an interactive live simulation of the Club of Rome's "Limits to
Growth" (the 1972 World3 study), the archetypal System Dynamics
feedback model singled out in Nelson's
.I Dream Machines
chapter on simulation.  Unlike this danby family's usual two- or
three-species ecology models, four stocks feed back on each other at
once: population, industrial capital, pollution, and nonrenewable
resources.
.SH MODEL
population grows from capital and resource availability and dies off
faster as pollution rises; capital is built from investment (itself
needing both population and resources) and lost to depreciation;
pollution is generated by capital's output and decays slowly;
resources are a fixed reserve drawn down by capital's extraction and
never replenished.
.SH DISPLAY
Each of the four stocks \(em population, capital, pollution, and
resources \(em is plotted as a separate coloured line against time,
System Dynamics' own characteristic chart, rather than the
two-variable phase portraits used by this family's simpler models.
.SH CONTROLS
The bottom tracks set the model's eight parameters: population
growth, base death rate, pollution mortality, investment rate,
capital depreciation, pollution generation, pollution decay, and
resource extraction.
.PP
Space pauses or resumes the simulation.  Period advances one displayed
step while paused.  The
.B r
key restores the default initial state and
.B q
or Escape exits.
.SH INTERPRETATION
The quantities are normalized unless physical units are stated
explicitly.  The display is a visualization of a low-dimensional
differential-equation model, not an agent simulation or a prediction
of a particular real economy or ecosystem.  Parameter changes restart
the displayed history so trajectories from different settings are not
joined.
.SH NUMERICS
The state is advanced by the general-purpose
.B Numerics
fourth-order Runge-Kutta solver.  Capital and resource factors saturate
smoothly toward zero as either runs out, avoiding a singularity at
zero rather than clamping.
